<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="dimension-6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Dimension 6: Promoting Social Justice</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This dimension aims to increase awareness about social justice issues through mathematical
      activities, analyzing the causes of the problems and brainstorming ideas to make progress
      on solving them, starting with personal stories from students, and moving to larger domains
      such as families, communities, the country, and the world.
    </p>

    <p>
      In our program, some of the mathematical activities include opportunities to raise awareness
      about different social justice issues that can relate at different levels to student's
      personal situations. These can be presented as games or simulations in which systemic
      injustice can emerge, showing real data that speaks about inequity, or through fictional
      situations that can later connect to the real world. We use scaffolded questions (sequences
      of 2<ndash/>3 carefully crafted questions) that gradually increase the depth of discussions,
      first involving student's experiences, then trying to capture the social issue more
      generally, and including key concepts and terms, and then giving space to reflections and
      connection to the mathematics learned.
    </p>

    <p>
      The peak of any activity is to connect to equity and social justice in deep ways such that
      the mathematics explored plays a key role in the discussion. This is quite challenging to do
      and likely won't happen in every activity, but this is the ultimate way for students to
      better understand the world around them and the ways that math can address real world social
      problems. Part of this is similar to holding students to high expectations where we want to
      empower students to believe that they are capable of making a difference, even if that
      happens in the future, or happens gradually. We are building awareness, at the very least.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="dim6-beliefs">
    <title>Mentors' Beliefs and Attitudes</title>

    <p><ul><li>I believe that <ellipsis/></li></ul></p>

    <p>Which other beliefs can shape your practice towards a teaching that promotes social justice?</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="dim6-actions">
    <title>Mentors' Actions</title>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>Educate students about fairness and social justice.</li>
        <li>Help students empathize with other people's struggles.</li>
        <li>Demonstrate how math can help address social justice issues.</li>
        <li>Encourage students to think about how they can improve the world around them.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="dim6-student-perspectives">
    <title>Students' Perspectives</title>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><q>I know more about global issues.</q></li>
        <li><q>I can understand how social justice issues impact individual people.</q></li>
        <li><q>I feel like I am capable of making the world a better place.</q></li>
        <li><q>I can apply my math knowledge to help understand social justice.</q></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

</section>
